\f1\fs22 After Sarah woke from deep sleep about an hour later than her usual time, and found Kathy missing, she pulled yesterday\'92s clothes on, and went downstairs. There was voices in the kitchen and the rich smell of bacon grease rising up the stairs. Sarah was surprised to feel hopeful; were there Elvi here?\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy\'92s voice was clear in the warble of voices. Perhaps the other one was Hayley\'92s. No, there were two more voices.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh hey, girl,\'92 Kathy said as Sarah eased sleepily into the kitchen. Kathy was flipping a pile of streaky bacon in a pan that was, though sizeable, too small for the task. On the other side of the kitchen, Hayley ground coffee beans for the espresso machine.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Standing at the island counter, Eloise was picking from a large cornucopia of fruit, slicing and tossing the chunks into a large blender cup. \'91Oh, you\'92re awake, good.\'92 Eloise said. \'91Fruit juice?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, please.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Within a few minutes, the kitchen hands had made the coffee, blended the juice, crisped the bacon and flipped flapjacks and found maple syrup in a stasis fridge the size of a small elevator. The four of them carted breakfast items out to a sturdy table on the patio. Sarah took only juice, while Hayley and Kathy ate heartily and Eloise picked at a piece of bacon and sipped weak tea from an expensive cup.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They made small talk, remarked about the freshness of the air, the scent of the flowers and the warbling of the magpies, until Hayley\'92s cup of patience ran dry. \'91How long do you plan on keeping us here?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I have no such plans, but you may consider keeping yourselves here until I can be sure PAPER is back on its chain,\'92 Eloise said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And who will put your son back on his chain?\'92 Hayley persisted, in no awe of Eloise. \'91Suppose that\'92s my problem?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Alexander will always be my problem,\'92 Eloise said sombrely, and cast a sympathetic glance at Sarah.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Do you think PAPER is acting on its own volition?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise dabbed at her lips with her napkin. \'91No. I think their motives go up higher than that. Even if they didn\'92t before, they will after my indelicate show of resistance yesterday. Piotr Dunhilton has invited me to tea.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They all sat quietly for a few moments, until Kathy spoke up. \'91Could I sneak back to my apartment and collect a few things?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Just make lists of everything you want. I\'92ll make sure you have it by sundown,\'92 Eloise said, accustomed to making small problems dissolve at the snap of her purse clasp.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yeah, it\'92s cold out there,\'92 Sarah remarked. \'91I haven\'92t brought 
\f2\i anything
\f1\i0  to wear.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There are coats and sensible shoes in the hall closet. You can make do today,\'92 Eloise said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s just,\'92 Kathy said, working her way up to explaining a specific need. \'91I have certain medicinal requirements \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes. Your patches,\'92 Eloise said. To her, this was just another business negotiation. \'91Whatever you need, I can provide at the highest quality.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, I dunno. I cook a lot of my own stuff.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley bristled. \'91Ah \'85 police officer here!\'92 And pointed to herself.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, of course,\'92 Kathy said, then to Eloise: \'91I need enough for all three of us.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley almost choked. Eloise smiled wryly. \'91It seems you want to party with the only clean police officer in all of Ulyssia.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy agreed. \'91I\'92m sure she\'92d be fun even without patching up.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley didn\'92t know whether to feel happy or appalled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m sure too,\'92 Eloise. \'91So make those lists. Clothes, jewellery, perfumes, gymnasium equipment, favorite teddy bears \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Food?\'92 Kathy asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92ll send someone down from catering to make sure you\'92re adequately stocked. You can even have one of my chefs, if you like.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley brightened. \'91Yes, please. All I can cook is coffee. Oh!\'92 A sudden thought. \'91I need all my computer gear from home. I still have much to investigate.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise regarded Hayley, realising she hadn\'92t wilted at all through all this. \'91No. I\'92ll get get you fresh hardware and load it with anything you need.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But I\'92ll need police department apps, authentication dongles\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Leave all that to me.\'92 She drain the last drops of her tea. \'91Sarah. Walk with me, will you?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ah, I could really use a shower \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m sure you could imagine the value I place on my own time, so if I\'92m willing to spend some of it with you, I would ask if you could be as gracious with yours. Besides,  I promise, we\'92ll stay outdoors.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\tx1660\tx2160\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl2\cf2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}Eloise led Sarah from the townhouse\'92s back yard into a large pentagonal garden featuring a large abstract fountain at its centre. Four other residences, larger than many penthouses of Airborne towers, were set around the central garden at tangential angles. The five boundaries of the central garden shared a section of about a third of a side boundary of the five buildings.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are any of these others occupied?\'92 Sarah asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Not at present.\'92 Eloise took Sarah through another of the yards, out across the roundabout, and to a beaten bush track Sarah couldn\'92t see until her feet were upon it. It wound around, predominantly away from the compound. Sarah saw no more buildings. Within a few minutes they emerged onto a clearing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah gasped as she tried to comprehend the vista. The previous evening, as they arrived in dusk, and the setting sun cast long, purple shadows over the ground, Sarah saw the landscape as a rolling rumpled carpet of bushland, a trick of the light that placed the compound on a plateau that extended for kilometres in every direction.  Perhaps it was the fantastic appearance of Eloise\'92s main residence that sapped all Sarah\'92s attention.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They walked onto bare rock. On both sides, sturdy hand railings corralled them towards the point of a wedge. The view was both ancient and immediate, as if some catastrophe had struck and a large section of the landscape had fallen away; like some god had taken to a rocky table and split a jagged chunk out of it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the railing, Sarah fought vertigo as she looked straight down to the canopy covering the valley floor hundreds of metres below. She looked to the horizon, to where the god\'92s table and its wound extended.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise put a hand on Sarah\'92s, a claw digging its nails into the timber railing. \'91Let\'92s have a seat.\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They followed the railing until a narrow path, with dizzying drops on both side, led a natural platform on which a park bench perched, corralled by more railing. Sarah was giddy as they walked out to it, steadying herself all the way with a hand on the railing, not fully trusting it could prevent her plummeting down to the verdantly cloaked ground. They sat, and Sarah gulped. And again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise rested her hand on Sarah\'92s, and gently massaged some relation into it. \'91It\'92s quite a sight, isn\'92t it?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92ve \'85 never \'85\'92 Sarah tried to explain. As an Airborne, she was never far from large windows over dizzying heights. But here \'85 \'91There\'92s no Layer.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How \'85?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Partly a blessing of geography. The valley is sheltered in the lee of the plateaus to the east, back towards the city. It would take quite a particular wind to blow the Layer over all that.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But not impossible.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No. But let\'92s just say I\'92ve deployed certain technologies that ensure the weather all around here is more to my liking, more natural.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shrugged, asking, 
\f2\i Is that it?
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I need this, Sarah, this pristine wilderness, all around, as far as the eye can see, as far away from my \'85 professional life as  practicable.\'92 Eloise lifted her gaze to the horizon. \'91To be so high, and yet to see the ground. Some find the experience almost spiritual. We live mostly without such stark, sensory evidence that the sky meets the earth.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah had an epiphany. She\'92d mostly lived without stark evidence of where the Airborne met the Cloudborne. Sure, she moved between the two worlds more than anyone she knew, but that was mostly play-acting, she saw\'97in rocky ochres, deep-green eucalypt, and stark, blinding blue. In truth, the Airborne rarely contemplated the ground; they were above the Layer, that\'92s all that mattered. The Layer served as an insubstantial, porous strata, a psychological ground that offered no true support\'97in fact, if you feel into on a bad day, it could eat you alive. Most Airborne bent little thought about what their above-Layer towers were built on, much less what lay under that: true bedrock, the real ground.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah surveyed with awe the rolling lush green gorges, and the sandstone ridges and mesas of yellow, ochre and muddy mulberry thrusting up like cracked pie crust. Her heart ached, overwhelmed with reconciliation and longing, longing for a part of herself that had been ripped away so long ago. She had no comprehension she was broken\'97un-whole\'97without it. Until now. 
\f2\i The Airborne have no wings
\f1\i0 , her inner dialogue told her. 
\f2\i They are no angels. They have feet meant to feel the ground, and the ground is there to cradle their weight. An inseparable connection. 
\f1\i0 Humanity is defined by the ground. To deny it is to deny nourishment, to starve the soul. Is it any wonder so many Airborne are psychopaths? They are disconnected in profound tragedy from the truth of their own gravity.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>If the Airborne would never grow wings, what of the Cloudborne? Well, they never need grow anything. Their feet were already on the ground. They were borne of the earth; not of the cloud, not of the toxic Layer. The Layer may cover them, and hide their faces of the misery from above, but they were enduring, in closer relation with the truth of the human condition. Perhaps they\'92d even thrive. They were not the Cloudborne; they were the Earthborne, and they would inherit the earth. The Airborne, never angels, would never grow wings, and they would plummet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise handed Sarah a handkerchief; it was only then that Sarah realised she was crying. \'91I\'92m sorry, I dunno why lately, I feel so \'85\'92 She wiped the corners of her eyes and dabbed around her nostrils.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, just blow your damn nose,\'92 Eloise chided. \'91We have a laundry on site, you know.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah sputtered a laugh. Bugled into the handkerchief.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Elegant,\'92 Eloise wryly critiqued.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah laugh again, longer and more easily, embarrassed. Some of the tensions eased from her chest.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise nodded to their right, towards something in the distance. \'91You see the point ever there? Just back a bit, those rock formations? They\'92re called the Three Sisters.\'92\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah squinted. The morning light seemed sharp and abrasive on the sandstone over there.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You see?\'92 Eloise asked again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I guess so.\'92 There seemed to be three lumps in ridge\'92s profile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, they\'92re more distinct when viewed from the west, from the Katoomba side.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91OK\'92, Sarah said, even though she had no understanding of what a 
\f2\i Katoomba side
\f1\i0  was.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They\'92ve been there for hundreds of millions of years,\'92 Eloise said. \'91The three of them, supporting each other. I suspect, if one of them ever cracks and crumbles, the other two would fall as well.\'92 Sarah frowned. Eloise chewed on her words. \'91I know you have this self identity of Little Miss Invincible, or whatever, but all that is just a warm-up for what lies ahead for you, I suspect. And you\'92ll need you supports. Your sisters.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Kathy?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise casts her vision over to the point. \'91I think you need one either side.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise erupted in full-bellied spontaneous laughter. She clasped her chest to calm herself, and gently coughed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Then?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Someone who has the temerity to stare down my son and who\'92s still staring me down.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah considered. \'91Hayley.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise nodded as she stared out over the valley. A breezed tousled her immaculate hairstyle; Eloise restored it with a casual wave of her hand. \'91I can sum up a person in two breaths. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Detective Inspector Hayley Lanning is police officer who\'92s not crooked, who\'92s fearless, thorough, resourceful \'85 and she\'92s got the hots for Kathy.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Given the right context, scope and motivation, she\'92ll be intensely loyal to the both of you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Really.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Indeed. I\'92m thinking of offering her a job. Kathy too.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And what would you have them do?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Anything you require.\'92\
\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92re not at all what I expected from a warlord.\'92\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise let the dysphemism pass. \'91That\'92s why I\'92m good at it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah considered that. \'91Are you offering me a job too?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91My darling, invincible Sarah. You can have a seat on the board, if you wish.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And jobs for Kathy and Hayley?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I don\'92t know if you\'92ve noticed, but Kathy has quite an advanced understanding of certain plant extracts and pharmaceuticals. And Hayley\'92s \'85 integrity \'85 would seem surplus to requirements for a position in your organisation.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sarah, I value integrity.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah frowned. \'91You killed one of your bodyguards\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Contractor.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91\'97right before me, just to scare me. Where\'92s the integrity in that?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Believe me, Sarah, hard decisions require more integrity, especially those that are harsh on more localised parts of the whole system.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah marvelled at the monstrousness of Eloise\'92s self justification. \'91How many of your employees die in your service?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise pursed her lips. \'91Year-on-year we\'92re down 3.4%,\'92 she fired. \'91Personnel is particularly pleased with that figure. The only thing they hate more than employee mortality is voluntary turnover.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They prefer deaths over resignations \'85\'92 Sarah processed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The dead don\'92t break non-compete clauses.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah saw the futility of challenging Eloise\'92s ethics, her morality. Perhaps even her humanity. \'91Why? Why me?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Because you are the mother of my one and only heir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Alexander can give you grandchildren.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise tossed her head. \'91Holy accountants preserve us!\'92 Her lips went momentarily thin while she composed herself. \'91I have few expectations of the world after I\'92ll have gone to the great second-hand arms market in the sky, but I am determined to ensure\'97and believe me, I say this with the greatest love for my Alexander. No issue of my son shall inherit any \'85\'92 She huffed. \'91Anything.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah sat quiet for a moment while she pouted. \'91So I\'92m just a meat-sack humidi-crib to you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise stabbed a look of scorn at Sarah. \'91If you were such, I\'92d merely put you in a coma until your baby was born, then discard you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Those words chilled Sarah. She knew Eloise was capable of such an atrocity, and had probably committed similar evil deeds countless times in the past. \'91Is that your back-up plan?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise let her head swing in slow arcs of refute. \'91Don\'92t wear at my patience, Sarah. Don\'92t test me. And never, ever, invite an audience with my darker angels.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah watched Eloise watching the horizon, and studied her jaw gently grinding emotional gristle between her molars. She knew if she didn\'92t say it now, she\'92d\'97\'91I can\'92t marry Alexander.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You can and you will. And we will all be better for it.\'92\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But\'97\'92 Sarah shuddered as she groped for words. \'91I can\'92t be a wife. I never wanted children. This one 
\f2\i must
\f1\i0  be the last.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise met Sarah\'92s gaze for the first time since they\'92d left the townhouse, and saw fearful dread in her eyes. \'91So be it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A small cloud drifted across the edge of the valley. Eloise scrutinised it for a moment and assessed it wasn\'92t an errant puff of the Layer straying across from the eastern side of the mountains. \'91Alexander doesn\'92t want a wife,\'92 she continued. \'91He wants a marriage, and the appearance of a wife. He wants another trophy\'97a shiny trophy he can worship and put on a pedestal for a while, and then toss in a closet.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, thanks for selling it. I feel much better.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The point is, he\'92ll move onto the next thing. Ironically, the best way to be free of him is to give him what he wants, and wait till he tires of it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And how long will that take?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Let me manage that.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And in the meantime? I can\'92t \'85 live \'85 with him.\'92 Sarah shuddered again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In her peripheral vision, Eloise saw it. She\'92s hones the skill of observing people and things without appearing to so so. \'91I\'92ll advise him you should stay here until after the birth. That should give us time. And he\'92s already biting on my international  syndication of 
\f2\i Candid Criminal.
\f1\i0  I could have him travelling the globe until well after Christmas\'97send him to Africa or Europe or \'85 Tajikistan or, I dunno \'85 Idaho.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You just keep giving him bigger trophies. Sooner or later\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I can outrun my son.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So a woman\'92s job is still to placate a man\'97even as his demands become more unreasonable?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Don\'92t be ridiculous, Sarah. That\'92s a mother\'92s job.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91A mother of sons.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise stood and stared Sarah down, her eyes fierce and her lips thin as blades. \'91Look Sarah, either work with me or throw yourself off this lookout right now. I\'92m tired of this.\'92 She took her thunderstorm of anger into the bush, back to the compound.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For many minutes after Eloise left, Sarah could feel the threat of her lightning strike.
